    "probe_text": "## 1. Concepts and rules, in dependency order\n\nPer-person values are computed on the server in `PersonView.java`. Sheet-wide totals and counts are computed in the browser in `people-list.ts`.\n\n**Raw numbers (`Person.java`)**\n1. `alpha`, `beta`, `gamma`: optional whole numbers. A blank one counts as zero in every calculation (`orZero`).\n2. `hold`: the \"on hold\" flag.\n3. `patrons`: the people this person names as patrons.\n\n**Before `keel`**\n4. `thrust` = alpha \u00d7 beta\n5. `momentum` = thrust + 3 \u00d7 (gamma \u2212 alpha)\n6. `scaled` = momentum \u00d7 2\n7. `third` = scaled \u00f7 3\n8. `quarter` = scaled \u00f7 4\n9. `tier` = `SINK` if scaled < 0, `STEADY` from 0 to 199, `SURGE` at 200 or more\n10. `averageScaled` (sheet-wide) = the average of scaled over people **not on hold** whose scaled is not n/a. It is null if nobody qualifies.\n11. `norm` = scaled \u2212 averageScaled\n\n**Patron aggregate**\n12. `mutualPatrons`: patronage is mutual. A person's patrons are the people they name plus the people who name them, each counted once. Naming yourself is ignored.\n13. `backing` = the sum of the patrons' momentum (n/a patrons add nothing). It is n/a when the person's own momentum is n/a.\n\n**Central value**\n14. `keel` = scaled + 2\u00b7momentum + 2\u00b7norm + 2\u00b7thrust. It is n/a when momentum or norm is n/a.\n\n**Computed from keel** (each is n/a when keel, or any other input it uses, is n/a)\n15. `fathom` = keel + momentum + 68\n16. `draft` = keel + thrust + 88\n17. `cascade` = fathom + scaled + 72\n18. `trim` = keel \u2212 thrust + 92\n19. `ripple` = cascade + keel + 76\n20. `heading` = keel + norm + 96\n21. `ballast` = ripple + thrust + 80\n22. `keystone` = ballast + norm + 84\n23. `bearing` = keel \u2212 norm\n24. `pitch` = keel + momentum; `tackle` = keel \u2212 momentum\n25. `yaw` = keel + scaled; `heave` = keel \u2212 scaled\n26. `camber` = keel + 200; `sheer` = keel \u2212 100\n27. `beam` = keel \u00d7 3; `freeboard` = keel \u00f7 3\n28. `buoyancy` = keel \u00d7 3; `knot` = keel \u00f7 3 (these duplicate beam and freeboard)\n29. `cable` = keel + gamma; `hull` = keel \u2212 gamma\n30. `bow` = keel + fathom; `stern` = keel \u2212 fathom\n31. `helm` = keel + ripple; `rudder` = keel \u2212 ripple\n32. `mast` = keel + 2000; `rigging` = keel \u00f7 4\n33. `moor` = keel + keystone; `berth` = keel \u2212 keystone\n34. `wharf` = keel \u00d7 5; `jetty` = keel \u00f7 10\n35. `pier` = keel + 500; `ebb` = keel \u2212 500\n36. `flood` = keel + ballast; `swell` = keel \u2212 ballast\n\n**Sheet-wide rules**\n- **Rounding (`SheetMath.divide`, copied as `divide` in the front end):** every division rounds to the nearest whole number, and a half rounds up toward the larger number (5/2 \u2192 3, \u22125/2 \u2192 \u22122).\n- **Holds:**\n  - People on hold are left out of `averageScaled`.\n  - In totals, they count for half their value (rounded with `divide`).\n  - They are left out of counts and of low/top/spread/mean.\n- **Trimming (`trimmedTotal`):** every \"\u2026 total\" adds up the non-n/a contributions. When at least two people contribute, it drops one highest and one lowest value. The halved on-hold values take part in that trimming.\n- **Which people count (`countable`):** every count (\"Keel count\", \"\u2026 even\") includes only people who are not on hold and whose keel is not below zero.\n- **Other footer values (low/top/spread/mean)** use only people not on hold and skip n/a values. The mean uses `divide`.\n\n## 2. How hard it was to follow\n\nThe logic is scattered and hard to follow:\n- **Split across tiers:** per-person values live on the server, in a 340-line `PersonView` record made of ~50 almost identical methods that recompute `keel` and its inputs again and again. Holds, trimming and counting live only in the Angular component, mixed in with copy-pasted footer code, and the rounding rule is implemented twice.\n- **Docs out of sync with the code:** the TS comments in `people.service.ts` say `draft` and `heading` have no constant, `beam` is \u00d72 and `freeboard` is \u00f72. The Java code (what actually runs) uses +88, +96, \u00d73 and \u00f73. A new developer couldn't trust the comments and would have to read both tiers."
